WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:04.580
For the first time ever, a "West Side Story"
movie has more than one Puerto Rican in the

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:05.580
cast.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.760
Here's everything else we loved about Steven
Spielberg's "West Side Story."

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.779
The Upper West Side of Manhattan was in a
state of flux back in the late 50s and early

00:00:13.779 --> 00:00:14.779
60s.

00:00:14.779 --> 00:00:18.880
Thanks to the so-called "urban renewal" programs
initiated by Robert Moses, who sought to beautify

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:23.539
New York City, many poor communities were
torn down, with residents displaced.

00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:27.019
The slums of the Upper West Side were high
in crime and a haven for youth gangs, hence

00:00:27.019 --> 00:00:30.380
why it was chosen as the setting for "West
Side Story" in the first place.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:34.010
Today, after decades of gentrification and
the building of Lincoln Center, the Upper

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:38.190
West Side is a neighborhood for New York's
more wealthy denizens, while its former residents

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:42.100
and their descendants found themselves driven
north to Harlem, Washington Heights, the Bronx,

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.789
and parts of Westchester.

00:00:43.789 --> 00:00:47.379
In the new "West Side Story," this looming
gentrification hangs over the neighborhood,

00:00:47.379 --> 00:00:51.660
adding an extra layer of tragedy to the story;
since the gangs are fighting over turf, but

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:55.410
that turf is about to be demolished by the
government, then they fight only for the sake

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:56.420
of their own hate.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:00.500
The very first scene features a sign advertising
the construction of Lincoln Center, and numerous

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:04.160
other scenes allude to the urban renewal that
will change the neighborhood forever.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:09.020
"You know I wake up to everything I know either
getting sold, or wrecked, or taken over by

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:10.300
people that I don't like."

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:15.750
While the 1961 film is still a beloved classic,
it nevertheless has some elements that don't

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:18.090
quite hold up in 2021.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:22.130
Most notably, the Puerto Rican characters
are played almost exclusively by white actors

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:23.130
in dark makeup.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:25.290
"Are there any questions?"

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:26.370
"Yes, sir.

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:28.630
Would you mind translating that into Spanish?"

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:33.630
The sole Puerto Rican in the cast, Rita Moreno,
had to wear "brownface" makeup to match her

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:34.630
darkened co-stars.

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:38.950
For the remake, the production enlisted the
help of actual Latinos to play the Puerto

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:39.950
Rican roles.

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:44.880
Maria is played by Rachel Zegler, who is half-Colombian,
while Bernardo's actor, David Alvarez, is

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:49.390
Cuban-Canadian, and Ariana DeBose, who plays
Anita, is Afro-Puerto Rican.

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:53.270
Also noteworthy is the amount of un-subtitled
Spanish dialogue in the film, the meaning

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:55.670
of which can be gleaned through context.

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:59.851
This reinforces the notion that "West Side
Story" isn't a Latino American story or a

00:01:59.851 --> 00:02:01.870
white American story -- it's both.

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:06.990
The Jets are a gang of white boys from the
street, which includes the character Anybodys.

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:11.189
Their characterization varies depending on
the production and is thus open to interpretation,

00:02:11.189 --> 00:02:15.250
but most versions are easy to interpret as
transgender characters.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:20.019
In the 1961 film, Anybodys, played by Susan
Oakes, is constantly dismissed by Riff and

00:02:20.019 --> 00:02:21.799
the Jets for being a girl.

00:02:21.799 --> 00:02:25.329
After some persistence they're eventually
accepted by Ice, the de facto leader of the

00:02:25.329 --> 00:02:26.510
Jets.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:27.700
"Hey!

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:31.709
Ya done good, buddy boy."

00:02:31.709 --> 00:02:34.810
"Thanks."

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:39.159
In the new film, Anybodys is played by Iris
Menas, a nonbinary actor.

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:42.980
They initially appear as something of a tagalong
character who follows the Jets more than they

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:44.690
actually interact with them.

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:48.209
Eventually, Anybodys gets locked up along
with the other Jets, and is placed in the

00:02:48.209 --> 00:02:52.579
women's side of the holding room, with the
more overtly male Jets on their own bench.

00:02:52.579 --> 00:02:56.959
When the Jets hassle Anybodys over their gender,
calling them a girl, Anybodys shouts, "I ain't

00:02:56.959 --> 00:03:01.739
no girl!" and pounces on the other gang members,
throwing punches that catch everyone off guard,

00:03:01.739 --> 00:03:02.939
even the cops.

00:03:02.939 --> 00:03:07.780
Overall, Anybodys has always been a revolutionary
character for trans theater enthusiasts, and

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:12.249
this new iteration reinforces that notion
without derailing the film from its main story.

00:03:12.249 --> 00:03:16.389
Anybodys is who they are, and that's that;
if you've got a problem with that, be ready

00:03:16.389 --> 00:03:17.389
for a fight.

00:03:18.389 --> 00:03:21.120
Tony and Maria first meet at a dance attended
by both gangs.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.079
Initially, the dance is willfully segregated,
with each gang dancing with their dates on

00:03:25.079 --> 00:03:27.060
their respective side of the gymnasium.

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:30.810
Even when the emcee tries to get the dancers
to intermingle, it doesn't take, and the dance

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:32.659
nearly breaks down into a brawl.

00:03:32.659 --> 00:03:36.670
The song, "Dance at the Gym," is played with
a near-manic energy beyond that of the original

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:40.099
movie, and the new dancing has extra energy
to match.

00:03:40.099 --> 00:03:43.379
Spielberg treats the dancers with the same
respect and dedication as the stuntmen in

00:03:43.379 --> 00:03:47.680
any "Indiana Jones" movie, drawing attention
to their incredible talent with long takes

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.760
and sharp camera movements that heighten the
dancing just like the best of his action/adventure

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:52.760
movies.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:56.700
When Maria and Tony notice each other and
make an instant love connection, the production

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:01.060
design kicks into overdrive, with the lighting
changing to reflect how the world simply looks

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:03.049
different when you're in love.

00:04:03.049 --> 00:04:07.340
This sense of romantic whimsy underscores
most of Tony and Maria's scenes and elevates

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:11.519
"West Side Story" beyond a mere remake of
its source material, but a brand new interpretation

00:04:11.519 --> 00:04:12.840
of a timeless love story.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:18.000
One of the biggest changes in the film from
the 1961 version is the path of the gun that's

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.000
ultimately used to kill Tony.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:24.500
In the 1961 movie, Chino just kind of has
the revolver, and it doesn't appear onscreen

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:27.370
until he uses it in the last several minutes
of the story.

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:30.980
Here, the gun is purchased by Riff and the
Jets to make sure they have their bases covered

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:32.112
in case the Sharks bring a gun.

00:04:32.112 --> 00:04:36.311
The shopkeeper from whom they purchase the
gun draws a parallel between their gun and

00:04:36.311 --> 00:04:41.260
the omnipresent danger — especially in 1957
— of nuclear annihilation.

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:45.220
Riff thinks he's buying a "deterrent," but
the seller makes reference to "mutually assured

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:46.220
destruction."

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:50.030
Deterrence is the idea that if both sides
build up enough of an armory, each side might

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:54.090
be too intimidated to escalate violence against
each other ... or, they might destroy each

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:55.090
other completely.

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:58.310
"Do you want to start World War III?"

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:02.070
During the rumble, Riff gives Tony the gun,
"just in case," but it's discarded during

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:03.100
the battle.

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:06.670
While the gang members flee from the carnage
they unleashed, Chino sees the six-shooter

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:11.240
lying on the ground and picks it up, setting
the stage for the film's final, tragic showdown.

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:16.660
In all versions of "West Side Story," the
rumble ends the same way: With Bernardo and

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:20.310
Riff dead on the floor after Tony tries in
vain to prevent the fighting.

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:23.660
This time around, there are some twists to
the proceedings, with Tony slicing off a bigger

00:05:23.660 --> 00:05:25.490
piece of the action than before.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:29.330
Usually, the climactic fight between Riff
and Bernardo kicks off when the latter insults

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:32.350
and hits Tony, who is only trying to stop
the fighting.

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:36.560
This time, Tony doesn't stick to his pacifist
intentions, and when Bernardo goads him, he

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:40.220
puts up his dukes and engages the Shark in
one-on-one combat.

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:43.710
Tony wins the fight, and the victory is all
the more impressive since this version of

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:45.280
Bernardo is a trained boxer.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:49.551
However, before delivering the finishing blow
on a subdued Bernardo, Tony is reminded of

00:05:49.551 --> 00:05:54.150
the boy he beat nearly to death, and how he
might do the same -- or worse -- to Bernardo.

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:56.310
Afterwards, he stops the fight.

00:05:56.310 --> 00:06:00.120
Unfortunately, this leaves an enraged Bernardo
to whip out a switch blade and rush the former

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:04.710
Jet leader with a renewed vigor, at which
point Riff steps in with a blade of his own.

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:09.120
From here, things play as expected, with Bernardo
accidentally killing Riff, and Tony killing

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:10.580
Bernardo in retaliation.

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:15.160
After the rumble, Maria is shown working at
the Gimbels department store, eager to see

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:19.430
Tony again — especially because she naively
believes he was able to defuse the tensions

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:20.860
between the two gangs.

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:23.900
She doesn't know that he failed and killed
her brother, Bernardo.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:27.870
In her unwitting ignorance, she breaks into
song and sings "I Feel Pretty," a beautiful

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:32.190
solo number about being young and in love,
with the rest of the world just melting away.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:36.550
In the 1961 movie, the song is moved up in
the story, placed before the rumble.

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:40.240
It's likely the decision was made either to
keep the tone from getting too light so soon

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:43.890
after the rumble, or to spare Maria from destiny's
cruel joke.

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:47.740
But with the new movie restoring "I Feel Pretty"
to its place from the original show, it adds

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:50.720
an extra layer of bittersweet tragedy to the
song.

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:54.770
Maria's life has been changed forever, and
it's about to get even worse, but she doesn't

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:55.870
know it yet.

00:06:55.870 --> 00:06:59.930
This way, when she finds out the truth from
Chino, the revelation is even more heartbreaking.

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:05.550
Rita Moreno, who played Anita in the 1961
movie, returns for the remake, this time playing

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:07.490
a new character, Valentina.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:11.280
She's the wife of Doc, the owner of the drug
store where Tony works, and effectively takes

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:12.750
his place in the narrative.

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:16.870
As a Puerto Rican woman married to a white
man, she has a unique perspective on the events

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:20.890
that unfold, and she sees the budding love
between Tony and Maria as something that,

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:24.180
while fraught with danger, could bring peace
to the dueling gangs.

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:27.510
When she learns of the deadly rumble, Valentina
is devastated.

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:31.440
She knows firsthand the potential of love,
but she also knows that hate can be such an

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:33.220
overwhelming force.

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:36.630
As she looks at pictures of herself and her
husband, Doc, she breaks into "Somewhere,"

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:39.200
one of the most beautiful songs from the musical.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:43.330
The song laments the hatred and violence that
surrounds Tony and Maria, but with Valentina

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:46.990
singing it, it becomes an anthem for interracial
love.

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:50.080
Valentina and Doc surely had their own "West
Side Story," and they were lucky enough to

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:52.790
make it through with their lives and their
love intact.

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:55.180
Tony and Maria would not have the same good
fortune.

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:59.890
Chino is something of a non-entity in previous
versions of "West Side Story."

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:03.570
He's in something of an arranged marriage
to Maria, but she's not interested in him

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:06.630
romantically — though she has no personal
grudge against him.

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:10.700
He's the one who tells Maria that Tony killed
Bernardo, and then shows up again at the end

00:08:10.700 --> 00:08:11.710
to shoot Tony.

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:14.770
He's more of a plot device than an actual
character.

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:18.760
The new movie gives purpose to Chino, with
more care and attention paid to his characterization

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:19.760
and motives.

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:20.760
Actor Josh Andrés Rivera imbues Chino with
a sheepish charm.

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:23.610
After the rumble, Chino picks up Riff's gun
and runs off alongside the rest of the Sharks.

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:27.640
He is the focal point of a new scene where
he laments the death of Bernardo, his best

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:28.640
friend.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:31.890
While the other Sharks wish for a full-scale
war against the Jets, Chino decries their

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:35.520
selfish pride and calls his dead friend a
fool who allowed his own pride to drag him

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:36.999
into an early grave.

00:08:36.999 --> 00:08:41.229
Nevertheless, he sets out to avenge his fallen
friend, knowing full well such an act will

00:08:41.229 --> 00:08:43.279
cost him his soul, and his freedom.

00:08:44.279 --> 00:08:48.639
After Maria and Anita reconcile, or at least
reach an understanding, Anita goes to Doc

00:08:48.639 --> 00:08:52.850
and Valentina's drug store to tell Tony to
wait for Maria to arrive so they can run away

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:53.850
together.

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:57.810
But when Anita arrives, she is accosted by
the Jets, who have become an angry, ravenous

00:08:57.810 --> 00:09:00.180
mob of young monsters.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:03.949
Valentina rescues her and shames the Jets
into disbanding, but the damage is already

00:09:03.949 --> 00:09:08.610
done, and Anita calls Valentina a race traitor
before lying and saying that Chino has killed

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:09.610
Maria.

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:13.650
﻿When Valentina tells Tony, he loses control
and runs into the night, begging for Chino

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:15.060
to finish him, too.

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:18.670
Chino then shoots Tony as Maria helplessly
watches.

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:23.019
After Tony dies in Maria's arms, Maria snatches
the gun from Chino's hands and threatens to

00:09:23.019 --> 00:09:27.860
shoot him, the Jets, the Sharks, and herself,
saying: "I can kill, too, because now I have

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:28.860
hate!"

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:32.699
It's a brutal declaration, and Rachel Zegler's
intensity matches that of Natalie Wood from

00:09:32.699 --> 00:09:34.149
the original film.

00:09:34.149 --> 00:09:37.930
Moved by her words and finally understanding
the consequences of their actions, members

00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:43.110
of both gangs respectfully carry Tony's body
away while the cops arrive to arrest Chino.

00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:47.110
Suffice to say, it's not a happy ending, and
Maria's survival deprives "West Side Story"

00:09:47.110 --> 00:09:51.660
of the conclusiveness of "Romeo &amp; Juliet,"
since the Jets and Sharks must now live forever

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:54.990
with the knowledge that their petty hatred
destroyed a love that had the potential to

00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:57.519
be more powerful than any street gang.

00:09:57.519 --> 00:09:59.860
